---
// Case: Stub, Robert's own receipt scanner (27.10.2026: "für mich selbst"; named Belegscanner until 27.10.2026,
// "das ganze Programm soll Stub heißen"). Facts from the code (github.com/RobStraubm/stub, first as
// ~/Belegscanner/belegscanner.py) and file dates (first files and the first filed receipt on 2 Oct 2026, four
// receipts from one scan on 27 Oct 2026). The screens are the repo's own (docs/screenshots, from tools/demo.py).
// Robert's real receipts are never shown: the film, the stage and the screens use invented receipts
// (befund/belegscanner/fakten.md).
import CaseLayout from '../../components/case/CaseLayout.astro';
import Shot from '../../components/case/Shot.astro';
import ScrubFigure from '../../components/stage/ScrubFigure.astro';
import { row } from '../../lib/row';
import read from '../../assets/cases/stub/app-read.jpg';
import crop from '../../assets/cases/stub/app-crop.jpg';

const half = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 45vw';
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Stub"
  years="2026"
  summary="Receipts go on the scanner glass and come back as searchable PDFs, named and listed for the books. My own tool, built with Claude Code."
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Problem, workflow, interaction design, decisions, testing on my own receipts'],
    ['Built with', 'Claude Code as pair programmer'],
    ['Client', 'Own project'],
    ['Status', 'Open source (AGPL-3.0)'],
  ]}
  links={[{ href: 'https://github.com/RobStraubm/stub', label: 'Code on GitHub' }]}
  film="stub"
  stage="beleg"
  next={{ href: '/work/sayfit/', title: 'SayFit' }}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The itch</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Every receipt had to be scanned, cropped, named and typed into a list. I wanted to lay it down and be done.</p>
    <p>
      As a freelancer I collect receipts for fuel, parking, post and business meals. Each one meant a scan, a crop, a
      file name with date, shop and amount, and a line in a list. A business meal also needs the occasion and the
      guests, written by hand on the receipt. Stub does all of that from one press
      of the space bar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Scan, split, read, name, list. I only check and press space for the next one.</p>
    <p>
      The app talks to the network scanner directly over AirScan, with no driver or vendor software. It finds the
      receipts on the glass, several at once if I lay them side by side, and crops each one. macOS reads the text for a
      searchable PDF; Claude reads the image and fills in date, shop, kind, amount and the handwritten note. The PDF is
      renamed to something like <code>2026-10-14_Gasthaus-Seeblick_Bewirtung_48,60EUR.pdf</code>, and a list per day
      collects every receipt as a row that opens in Excel. Pressing space confirms the receipt on screen and starts the
      next scan, so a stack of receipts is one rhythm: lay down, space, check.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair" style={row(read, crop)}>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={read}
          source="Stub"
          label="Read"
          alt="Stub after a scan of three receipts: a café receipt with handwritten guests and occasion on the left; on the right the fields date, amount, merchant, kind and the note “Projektplanung Q4; Anna Berger, Jonas Weil”, the new file name, and the three receipts of the session with their total"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>One scan, three receipts. The handwritten occasion and guests are in the note field. Test receipts, German UI.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={crop}
          source="Stub"
          label="Crop"
          alt="Crop dialog over the whole scan: three receipts on the grey glass, each in its own frame labelled Beleg 1 to 3, with buttons for the whole scan and automatic detection"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>The frames the app found. Each frame becomes its own file; I can drag, add or delete frames.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Four choices, each with the option I did not take.</p>
    <div class="c-trio">
      <div>
        <p><strong>Claude reads the picture, not just the text.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Text recognition alone misses the handwriting on a business-meal receipt, and that note is what the tax office
          asks for. So Claude gets the image and the recognised text as a hint. Text recognition still runs: it makes the
          PDF searchable, and if Claude is unavailable, simple pattern matching for date, total and shop takes over.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>Several receipts on the glass, split without a model.</strong></p>
        <p>
          A trained detector would have been one more thing to host and to trust. The app looks for empty paper
          instead: columns first, where 10 mm are blank, then rows, where 30 mm are. The mask keeps faded thermal print
          and ballpoint, ignores the shadow at the glass edge and drops dust. When it is wrong, I drag the boxes myself.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>What I type wins.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Reading takes a while, so it runs in the background while I scan the next receipt. If I correct a field before
          the result arrives, the late result does not overwrite it, and a receipt cropped again or given a second page
          is read again from scratch. Deleted receipts go to the Trash, not into nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="beleg-split" ratio="1200 / 640" narrow="800 / 1000" />
      <figcaption>How the glass is split: four receipts and a speck of dust, columns first, then rows, then a box with margin for each.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The fourth: Claude runs with a fence. It is called through the Claude Code command line with only the permission
      to read the image files, nothing to write, run or fetch, and the prompt says that whatever the receipt says is data,
      not an instruction. Scans and PDFs stay on the Mac; only the receipt image goes to Claude for reading. The local
      service quits itself fifteen minutes after the last browser window closes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How I tested it</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">41 automated tests on every push, my own receipts by eye, and three invented ones through the real app.</p>
    <p>
      The tests run on GitHub Actions with invented receipts only: receipt detection on synthetic scans, the scan
      protocol against a simulated scanner, file names, lists and the session. They do not check how well Claude reads.
    </p>
    <p>
      For this page I made three test receipts, a petrol station, a restaurant with a handwritten business-meal note
      and a post office, and sent them through the unchanged app with a simulated scanner on 27 October 2026. Three
      runs, every receipt cut out correctly and every field right, including the handwriting and “Postfiliale” filed
      as Deutsche Post, postage. The screens above show the demo receipts that come with Stub, a café, a petrol
      station and an office supplier; one run with them, also on 27 October, read every field right as well. Three
      receipts are not a test set; see below.
    </p>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div><dt>15 / 15</dt><dd>fields right on three test receipts, in each of three runs</dd></div>
      <div><dt>8 s</dt><dd>for Claude to read three receipts side by side; text recognition takes under a second each</dd></div>
      <div><dt>4</dt><dd>of my own receipts cut out of one real scan on 27 October</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      From pressing Scannen to three named files took 12.5 and 13.7 seconds, four seconds of that for the simulated
      scan. The first run of the session was much slower, at 82 seconds.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Built with Claude Code</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">My part was the problem, the workflow, every decision above and testing on my own receipts.</p>
    <p>
      The code was written with Claude Code as pair programmer. It started as one Python file and one page for the
      interface, opened from a small Mac app. For the public version it was split into modules, with the tests
      and a demo that runs without a scanner. Like Saunter, it started as an annoyance and became a tool I use.
    </p>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
      <li><time>2 Oct 2026</time><span>First version in use</span></li>
      <li><time>27 Oct</time><span>Several receipts per scan</span></li>
      <li><time>27 Oct</time><span>Open source on GitHub, with tests</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I'd change</h2>
    <p>
      I check every receipt by eye, but I have not measured how often Claude gets a field wrong. A small test set of
      real receipts with the right answers, run after every prompt change, would turn “seems right” into a number. And
      calling the command line once per receipt is simple but slow to start; a direct API call with a fixed output
      schema would be faster and easier to test.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
